{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011Ccy3aKQbwzqHhX5fY2XZ7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've implemented the mushroom jump pad. Here's what I did.\n\n## Implementation\n\nThe pad (`jumping_pad/jumping_pad.gd`) was an empty `Area3D`. I filled it in:\n\n**Launch (physics response):** On `body_entered`, I set `body.velocity` directly. Because the player is a `CharacterBody3D` driven by `move_and_slide()`, overwriting its velocity is a real physics impulse — mid-air collisions with ceilings/walls still resolve naturally, exactly as the spec requires.\n\n**How each requirement is met:**\n1. **Instant, physics-based launch** — velocity is set the moment contact fires; no scripted position tween on the character.\n2. **Powerful** — `launch_force = 32`. The player's gravity is −30 and a normal jump impulse is 12 (~2.4 m). 32 launches to ~17 m, clearly far beyond any normal jump.\n3. **Momentum-independent + air control** — overwriting `velocity` (rather than adding to it) discards whatever momentum the player arrived with, so walking, sprinting, or falling in all produce the same bounce. Air steering is preserved automatically because the player's `_physics_process` keeps lerping horizontal velocity toward input every frame.\n4. **Surface-facing direction** — launch is along `global_transform.basis.y` (the pad's up/normal), so a flat pad throws straight up and a tilted pad throws up-and-outward.\n5. **Cap squash & rubbery rebound** — on each launch the mushroom instantly snaps to a squashed scale `(1.3, 0.55, 1.3)` (its mesh pivot is at the base, so the cap visibly presses down), then a `TRANS_ELASTIC`/`EASE_OUT` tween springs it back, wobbling past the rest shape before settling. Each contact kills any running tween and restarts it.\n6. **Player-only** — a `body is Player` guard means enemies, projectiles, boxes, etc. pass through with no launch and no visual reaction (the pad is an `Area3D`, so nothing is physically blocked either).\n\nThe tunables (`launch_force`, `rebound_time`, `squash_scale`) are `@export`ed for easy feel-tuning, and rest scale is captured at `_ready` so nothing else about the scene changes. I ran the project through the Godot MCP server — it launches with **no parse or runtime errors**.\n\nOne note: the three pads placed in `main.tscn` are rotated about the Y axis, so their surface normal is still straight up (they launch vertically); the outward-launch behavior kicks in for any pad actually resting on a tilted slope."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":456,"cache_read_input_tokens":38397,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":456,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8944ab55-e53f-4d35-860d-5d7583d8e338","uuid":"eded0f6a-c65e-43be-a57a-d8fd5fca6eed","request_id":"req_011Ccy3aJXmrAHE8zqd4Uzv6"}
